# Engineering

Details: `docs/conventions.md`. C# style: `.cursor/rules/csharp.mdc`. Tests: `.cursor/rules/testing.mdc`.

## Layout

- Solution: `ElasticLM.slnx` only. Do not commit a `.sln`.
- Projects follow `docs/conventions.md`: `src/ElasticLM.*`, `tests/*.Tests`, and `samples/`, `benchmarks/`, `tools/` when a row needs them. Do not create a project, folder, script, or skill step before the Issue that needs it.
- A project's line in `docs/conventions.md` is added in the PR that adds the project.
- The admin UI is `web/admin/` (design §15.2): Preact and TypeScript, built by Vite with pnpm through Corepack, on the Node.js of `web/admin/.node-version`. Its `dist` is not committed; `ElasticLM.Service` embeds it and does not build without it.

## Targets and build

- `net10.0` everywhere (`Directory.Build.props`). C# 14. Nullable on. `InvariantGlobalization` on.
- `TreatWarningsAsErrors` is on from the first commit. Do not add `NoWarn` or `#pragma warning disable` to get a build through; fix the cause, or change `.editorconfig` with the reason in a comment when a rule is wrong for this repository, in a PR that says so.
- Projects under `src/` are `IsAotCompatible` (`.cursor/rules/aot.mdc`) and need XML docs on the public API.
- Package versions live in `Directory.Packages.props` (Central Package Management). A new package goes through `.cursor/rules/dependencies.mdc` first.
- Projects under `src/` build with `BannedSymbols.txt`. Do not remove an entry to get a build through.

## Format and analyzers

- CSharpier is the only formatter (`.config/dotnet-tools.json`, `.csharpierrc.json`). It also formats `.csproj` and `.props`. Run `dotnet csharpier format .` before a commit.
- StyleCop.Analyzers and the .NET analyzers at `latest-recommended` run in every build. `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild` is on.
- Do not use an alignment in an interpolated string (`{x,5}`): CSharpier writes it `{x, 5}`, which StyleCop's SA1001 refuses. Pad with `PadLeft` instead.

## Scripted edits

A script that replaces text in a source file checks that its anchor occurs exactly once and stops otherwise. Where the same lines repeat in several functions, the anchor includes the function's declaration. In M4a3 a script anchored on lines that two handlers shared, rewrote the wrong one, and broke the file, which was restored from `HEAD` (PR #59).

A Python script on Windows that opens a file in text mode writes CRLF line ends. Open it with `newline=""` on both the read and the write, so the file keeps LF (M4b1, PR #61).

## Data access (AuraORM)

- A string primary key column has `[MaxLength(n)]`. Without it the generator makes no `Table` for the record and reports nothing. In M4d1 `LeaseReturnRow` had no table until its `Counter` key had a length (PR #83).
- A sum or a count is an aggregate in SQL (`Sum`, `Count`), not rows read and added in C#: the rows bring every column, a token included, and the read grows with the rows (PR #83).

## Changing a stored row

An operation that applies a request to a stored row (a `PATCH`, a state change) reads that row in the write transaction that writes it, and on PostgreSQL locks it first (`SELECT … FOR UPDATE`, or an `UPDATE` that sets a column to itself with `RETURNING`). Read outside the transaction, two changes of different members at once write back each other's old values. In M4a2 the first version of the `PATCH` endpoints did that, and the review found it (PR #55).

## Gate

`./build.ps1` before a PR. It builds the admin UI first (`web/admin/`: the Node.js and Corepack check, `corepack pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`, on Windows the path length check, `tsc`, Vitest, and the Vite build), then runs tool restore, the CSharpier check, restore, then for Release and for Debug a build and every test project. Release is what ships; Debug runs the checks that exist only there. Hosted CI builds the admin UI once in a job without secrets and runs the same script with `-SkipWeb` on Windows, Linux, and macOS.

A PR that makes a setting required for a server to start adds it, in the same PR, to every step of `.github/workflows/ci.yml` that starts that server (the NativeAOT smoke and measure steps). The gate starts the server from its tests and does not see the missing setting. In M4b1 the nonces' secret became required and the NativeAOT jobs failed on all three OS (PR #61).

A push that only asks CI to try something still builds the changed projects in Release first (with the analyzers) and runs the CSharpier check. In M5a a missing blank line before a comment (SA1515) stopped the gate on all three OS (PR #105).

A commit and a push follow the build only through its exit code. Piping the build's output into `grep` and chaining `git commit ... && git push` after it runs the commit even when the build failed. Write `if dotnet build ...; then git commit ...; fi`, or check `$LASTEXITCODE` in PowerShell.

If `dotnet --list-sdks` does not show a 10.0 SDK, install it; do not stop to ask.

A NativeAOT publish on Windows needs the MSVC linker (Visual Studio's "Desktop development with C++" workload) and `C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio\Installer` on `PATH`: Visual Studio's environment scripts call `vswhere.exe` by name. Add it for the session (`$env:PATH += ';C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio\Installer'`) before `dotnet publish`. If `dotnet csharpier` says to run `dotnet tool restore` right after a successful restore, delete `%USERPROFILE%\.dotnet\toolResolverCache\1\csharpier` and restore again.
